    {
      "role": "candidate",
      "content": "When working with Building the Knowledge Base, the main problem wasn’t about storing information—it was about structuring and organizing knowledge so it could be retrieved and applied effectively.\n\n🔑 Core Problem\nThe challenge was:\n\nFragmented information → data scattered across sources, formats, and contexts.\n\nUnusable knowledge → even if information was stored, it wasn’t actionable without proper indexing or categorization.\n\nScalability → as the knowledge base grew, retrieval speed and relevance became harder to maintain.\n\nSo the real issue was: How do we transform raw information into a structured, queryable, and useful knowledge system?\n\n🛠️ Main Tools & Techniques\nThe focus was on tools and methods that made knowledge organized, searchable, and contextual:\n\nOntology & taxonomy design → defining categories, hierarchies, and relationships between concepts.\n\nEmbedding-based search → using vector representations (e.g., sentence embeddings) to retrieve semantically similar information.\n\nMetadata tagging → attaching attributes like source, date, reliability, and domain to each entry.\n\nKnowledge graphs → linking entities and relationships for richer context.\n\nIterative curation → continuously refining entries, pruning duplicates, and updating outdated facts.",
      "question_meta": null
